Simple Web Stack
================

Overview
--------
This is a one-server web infrastructure that hosts the website
www.foobar.com. It is composed of a single server running a LAMP-style
stack: Nginx (web server), an application server, the application code,
and a MySQL database.

User flow
---------
1. A user opens a browser and types www.foobar.com.
2. The browser asks a DNS resolver to translate www.foobar.com into an
   IP address.
3. The DNS resolver returns the IP address 8.8.8.8 (the IP associated
   with the www A record for the foobar.com domain).
4. The browser sends an HTTP(S) request to 8.8.8.8.
5. Nginx (the web server) on the server receives the request.
6. Nginx forwards the request to the application server.
7. The application server executes the application code, which may
   query the MySQL database to retrieve or store data.
8. The application server returns a response to Nginx, which sends it
   back to the user's browser.
9. The browser renders the website for the user.

Components
----------
- Domain name: foobar.com, with a "www" record pointing to 8.8.8.8.
- 1 server, which contains:
  - 1 web server: Nginx
  - 1 application server
  - 1 codebase (application files)
  - 1 database: MySQL

Explanations
------------
What is a server?
A server is a physical or virtual machine that provides a service -
in this case hosting a website - to other machines (clients) over a
network.

What is the role of the domain name?
A domain name (foobar.com) is a human-readable name that maps to an IP
address, so users don't need to remember numeric addresses to reach a
website.

What type of DNS record is "www" in www.foobar.com?
It is an A record, which maps a hostname to an IPv4 address (8.8.8.8).

What is the role of the web server?
The web server (Nginx) accepts incoming HTTP/HTTPS requests from
clients and either serves static content directly or forwards the
request to the application server for dynamic content.

What is the role of the application server?
The application server executes the business logic of the
application - running the code that generates dynamic content,
communicating with the database if needed, and returning the response
to the web server.

What is the role of the database?
The database (MySQL) stores, organizes, and retrieves the data used
by the application (e.g. user accounts, articles, products).

What is the server using to communicate with the user's computer?
The server communicates with the user's computer using the HTTP (or
HTTPS) protocol over TCP/IP.

Issues with this infrastructure
--------------------------------
SPOF (Single Point of Failure):
Because there is only one server, if it goes down (hardware failure,
crash, etc.), the entire website becomes unavailable - there is no
redundancy.

Downtime when maintenance is needed:
Since the web server, application server, and database all live on
the same machine, any maintenance (e.g. deploying new code, restarting
the web server) requires taking the whole stack down temporarily,
causing downtime for users.

Cannot scale if there is too much incoming traffic:
A single server has a fixed amount of CPU, memory, and network
capacity. If traffic increases beyond what that server can handle,
there is no way to add more capacity (no load balancing, no
additional servers), so the website will slow down or become
unresponsive.
